<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/intermediate/line-shape-and-value">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Line, shape and value are the <strong>mechanics of seeing</strong> — the drawing lesson\'s
    three levers as trainable skills. This is the practice layer: drills, checks and the habits
    that make the eye honest.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="line, shape, value, edges — the four fundamentals" widget="equationStep" config={lsvCfg} />

  <h2>Practice that actually improves</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gesture drawing daily:</strong> 30-second and 2-minute figures — quantity builds the seeing-before-drawing reflex (the training lesson\'s skill acquisition: frequent, immediate-feedback reps beat occasional masterpieces).</li>
    <li><strong>Value studies in two tones:</strong> notan (light/dark only) studies force the structure question — if the design fails in 2 values, 20 won\'t save it.</li>
    <li><strong>Draw what you see, not what you know:</strong> the classic exercise — copy an upside-down master drawing; without recognisable "face" or "hand" labels, you finally draw the actual shapes (the perception lesson\'s top-down priors, deliberately jammed).</li>
    <li><strong>Materials are neutral:</strong> pencil, ink, pixel — the fundamentals transfer; each medium\'s friction teaches something different (the timbre lesson\'s trade: medium is voice, fundamentals are language).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Edwards, Drawing on the Right Side of the Brain; Nicolaïdes, The Natural Way to Draw; the value lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
